# You Cannot Retarget Grief

On a Friday morning in March, at eight forty-seven a.m., an eleven-year donor to your literacy program opens her inbox at the kitchen counter and finds three things stacked in the same pane. A PDF from the funeral home in the next town over with the order of service for her husband's memorial on Saturday. A note from her sister, who is driving up from Rhode Island. And, in the middle, the second email in your spring renewal sequence — subject line *One week left, Mary* — auto-sent by the platform at exactly the hour the vendor's playbook says produces the strongest open rate.

The email is well-designed. The subject line is above-industry-benchmark. The kids on the summer-camp banner are smiling. The ask string is the amount the model determined she is most likely to give.

The model was right about the amount, and wrong about every other thing that has ever mattered.

She unsubscribes on Sunday afternoon, from the parking lot of the Congregational church, on her phone, in the ten quiet minutes between the reception and driving her sister to the train.

The sequence performed exactly as designed. The relationship is over.

## The instinct we imported without meaning to

Marketing automation is one of the great inventions of e-commerce. It rescued the small online store from having to remember every customer by hand. It made it possible for a two-person Shopify seller to send a mother-of-the-groom the right belt in the right size on the right Tuesday morning without ever meeting her.

That is the good it did. It is not a small good. Nobody in fundraising is trying to argue against belts arriving on Tuesday.

The trouble is that the field, sometime around 2015, quietly imported the *sequencing instinct* — cart abandonment, drip campaigns, behavior triggers, urgency countdowns, retargeting pixels — and pointed it at donors as though the two audiences were shaped alike.

They are not shaped alike. A donor is not a cart. A grieving widow is not a lapsed shopper. A canceled monthly gift is not an abandoned checkout. The e-commerce loop's central assumption — *the customer wants the thing and simply needs to be reminded* — is a claim about desire that donors do not make.

Donors are not shopping. They are remembering. They are honoring somebody. They are trying to make the town less lonely.

The reminder mechanism, pointed at that, does not accelerate the giving. It contaminates it.

## The moments the sequence makes worse

There is a small honest list, kept in the notebooks of every planned-giving officer over sixty who has ever had to call a vendor and beg them to pause a queue by hand at nine at night, of the moments in a donor's life when the sequence is not neutral, it is *net-negative:*

The week after a spouse's death.

The month after a diagnosis.

The Friday after a grandchild's stillbirth.

The August she moved her mother into memory care and the March she moved her mother's things into a storage unit in Lebanon.

The second Sunday after the daughter's divorce came through.

The Wednesday after the layoff.

The Tuesday she was told the biopsy was benign, and the Tuesday she was told it wasn't.

The four to six weeks after a house fire.

The week between the eviction notice and the temporary shelter, if you are one of the many nonprofits whose smallest donors are also, at moments, in your own service population.

In every one of these windows, a well-designed, high-open-rate, $75-ask, sequenced email is not a neutral touch. It is a small clumsy hand on a shoulder that is already flinching. And the shoulder remembers, for a long time, which hand was there and which hand was not.

## What the CRM cannot know, and why nobody told it

The vendors are not evil. Nobody in the platform-marketing team at your fundraising CRM woke up on Monday and decided to send Mary the ask on the Friday her husband died. The system did what the system was told to do by defaults set in 2014 by a very smart product manager who was measuring open rates on a segment of small-dollar donors in Denver.

The CRM does not know that Mary's husband died. It has no field for that. There is no *bereavement flag* the funeral home in the next county over is going to push to your Salesforce instance on Wednesday morning. There is no LinkedIn signal for the biopsy result. There is no zap for the grandchild.

The information exists in the town. It exists on the pastor's counter, and the neighbor's kitchen table, and the small folded card the choir director dropped at the post office on Tuesday. It exists everywhere except the field on the record.

The sequence, meanwhile, runs on the fields that do exist — last gift date, RFM score, campaign membership — because those are the fields the vendor made available in the segment builder in 2014, and the segment builder in 2014 was designed for a lapsed-cart flow.

You cannot retarget grief because grief was never in the schema.

## The small unfashionable practice

There is a practice, kept quietly by the best mid-career development directors we know, that has no software product attached to it and no conference session named after it. It is a Monday-morning ritual, twenty minutes at the coffee pot, in which the director opens a plain notebook and writes down the names of the seven to twelve donors she thinks might be having a hard week.

She does not know for sure. She does not have a data source. She has a memory of the last conversation, a note about a spouse's health from October, a small honest awareness that a person she has known for eleven years is somewhere in a season that the newsletter is not going to help.

Beside each name, she writes one of three things.

*Pause.* Which means: gently take this donor out of the automated queues this month. No email sequence. No pledge-reminder cadence. No spring-appeal auto-send. Do not disappear on her — the office is still here — but do not shove a form into her hands while she is walking through the parking lot with a program in her coat pocket.

*Pen.* Which means: on Wednesday afternoon, write her a two-sentence card. Not a template. Not a printed signature. Two real sentences, in real ink, about the specific thing you know is happening in her life, without a single reference to the annual campaign. Then drop it in the mailbox on the corner of Main and Elm on the way to your two-thirty.

*Porch.* Which means: this donor is close enough that the right thing is not a card, it is a Saturday-morning visit. Bring a plant. Do not stay long. Say what she needs to hear, which is that you noticed, and that the organization is not, in this hour, going to ask her for anything.

That is the whole practice. Twenty minutes. A notebook. Seven to twelve names.

It has never, in any of the offices we know that have run it consistently for five years or more, produced a *decrease* in the following year's giving from those seven-to-twelve donors.

It has, in every one of those offices, produced the note in the file — often quoted at the retirement dinner, sometimes at the memorial — that reads *she remembered.*

## What a tool can quietly do here

Rōmy does not write the card. The director does. The specific sentence about the husband who taught seventh-grade shop for thirty-two years is *her* sentence — it has been in her memory since a Saturday in 2019 when Mary told her at a garden party and she wrote it down on the back of a napkin and put the napkin in the drawer.

What a tool can do, quietly, on a Monday morning at the coffee pot, is hand the director the short list.

Not a segment. Not a score. A short careful page for each of the seven-to-twelve names — the eleven-year giving history, the note about the husband's diagnosis from October, the funeral home's obituary posted on Wednesday, the small quiet flag *do not include in any sequence this month*, and the sources for each of those things so the director can trust what she is reading before she picks up the pen.

Then the director writes seven-to-twelve small cards that do not sound like seven-to-twelve small cards. They sound like one person, writing to one person, about one specific loss the recipient did not know anybody in the office had noticed.

The automation, meanwhile, keeps doing what it is genuinely good at — the acquisition emails, the year-end nudge to the donor who has been meaning to give, the receipt on the same afternoon as the gift. Those flows are, in the right lane, small useful mercies.

The lane is not, and has never been, grief.

## A quiet ask, in place of a CTA

We are not asking you to turn off your automation this quarter. We are not asking you to fire your vendor. We are not asking you to rewrite the spring appeal.

We are asking you, on the Monday after you read this, to open a plain notebook at the coffee pot, and, for twenty minutes, write down the seven-to-twelve names.

Write *pause* beside three of them. Write *pen* beside two. Write *porch* beside one.

Then, before you sit down at your laptop for the ten o'clock, do the one small unfashionable thing you wrote beside the first name.

The queue will still be there at eleven.

Mary will not be, if it isn't. ♡
